<ContentPage title="Risk Disclosure" subtitle="What can go wrong, and what we're doing about it." description="BARK project risk disclosure — honest assessment of project, financial, location, community, and legal risks.">
  <p>
    We believe transparency about risk is a prerequisite for informed participation.
    This page is intentionally blunt. If you're considering joining BARK in any capacity,
    read this first.
  </p>

  <h2>Project Risks</h2>
  <p>
    BARK is an early-stage project with a small founding team and no guaranteed outcomes.
    The vision is ambitious and the execution depends on attracting the right people,
    securing funding, navigating foreign legal systems, and building physical infrastructure
    from scratch. Most ambitious projects fail.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Team risk</strong> — Two co-founders. Key person dependency is real. If either leaves, the project's direction could change significantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Funding risk</strong> — No institutional funding secured. Bootstrap phase. Dependent on early participants contributing capital and labor.</li>
    <li><strong>Execution risk</strong> — Building off-grid infrastructure in a developing country is hard. Timelines will slip. Costs will exceed estimates.</li>
    <li><strong>Scale risk</strong> — The project needs to reach a critical mass of participants to be viable. If we can't attract enough people, some components won't be feasible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Financial Risks</h2>
  <p>
    Any capital contributed to BARK should be considered at risk. This is not an investment
    with guaranteed returns.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capital at risk</strong> — Cooperative shares may have limited liquidity. There may be no secondary market for your membership share.</li>
    <li><strong>Currency risk</strong> — The Bolivian boliviano (BOB) fluctuates against major currencies. Operating costs are in BOB; income may be in USD/EUR.</li>
    <li><strong>No guaranteed returns</strong> — Cooperative membership is participation, not investment. Financial returns depend on the community's economic success.</li>
    <li><strong>Sunk cost</strong> — Physical infrastructure and land improvements may not be easily liquidated if the project dissolves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Location Risks</h2>
  <p>
    Coroico is beautiful and strategically chosen, but it's not without challenges.
    These are the realities of building in the Yungas.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Landslides</strong> — The Yungas region is prone to landslides, especially during the rainy season (November–March). Road closures happen regularly.</li>
    <li><strong>Road access</strong> — The road from La Paz to Coroico, while improved, can be closed by weather or landslides. Isolation is a real possibility for days at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Limited healthcare</strong> — Basic hospital in Coroico. Serious medical emergencies require evacuation to La Paz (~3 hours). This is a genuine safety concern.</li>
    <li><strong>Altitude effects</strong> — At 1,750m Coroico is moderate, but La Paz airport (4,061m) is the entry point. Altitude sickness affects visitors.</li>
    <li><strong>Political instability</strong> — Bolivia has experienced political upheaval. While rural Yungas is generally calm, national instability can affect supply chains, regulations, and security.</li>
    <li><strong>Bureaucratic challenges</strong> — Government processes in Bolivia can be slow, opaque, and require local knowledge and connections to navigate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Community Risks</h2>
  <p>
    Intentional communities have a high failure rate. According to research from the
    Fellowship for Intentional Community and studies published in the
    <em>Journal of Sustainable Development</em>, the majority of intentional communities
    do not survive their first five years. Common causes: governance conflicts, financial
    disagreements, and interpersonal friction.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Governance conflicts</strong> — Decision-making in community settings is hard. Consensus breaks down. Factions form. Power dynamics emerge.</li>
    <li><strong>Personality clashes</strong> — Living in close community amplifies interpersonal friction. Small annoyances become major conflicts.</li>
    <li><strong>Commitment gaps</strong> — Some participants will commit less than others. Free-rider problems are real in cooperative settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Vision drift</strong> — Over time, the community's priorities may shift away from the original vision. This is natural but can cause fractures.</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural integration</strong> — Foreigners building a community in Bolivia must navigate cultural differences with respect and humility. Getting this wrong is disqualifying.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Legal Risks</h2>
  <p>
    Operating in Bolivia's legal system requires specialized knowledge and local legal counsel.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Land law complexity</strong> — Bolivian land law restricts foreign ownership and has specific rules for agricultural and rural land. Legal structure must be carefully designed.</li>
    <li><strong>Foreign ownership restrictions</strong> — Foreigners face limitations on land ownership. The cooperative structure is designed to address this, but legal challenges are possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Cooperative governance learning curve</strong> — Ley 356 (Bolivia's cooperative law) has specific requirements for governance, reporting, and member rights that must be followed precisely.</li>
    <li><strong>Regulatory changes</strong> — Laws can change. A future government could impose new restrictions on foreign participation, cooperative structures, or land use.</li>
    <li><strong>Dispute resolution</strong> — Legal disputes in Bolivia can be slow and unpredictable. International arbitration may not be available for all matters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What We're Doing About It</h2>
  <p>
    Identifying risks without mitigation is just anxiety. Here's what we're doing for each category.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project risks</strong> — Expanding the founding team (reducing key-person dependency), maintaining everything as open source (the project can continue without any individual), phased development (each phase delivers standalone value).</li>
    <li><strong>Financial risks</strong> — Conservative budgeting, phased investment (don't spend what you haven't raised), diversified income streams (trading house, incubator, retreats), clear financial reporting to all members.</li>
    <li><strong>Location risks</strong> — Emergency supply reserves, medical evacuation plan, Starlink for communications redundancy, relationships with local emergency services, building to seismic and landslide-resistant standards where possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Community risks</strong> — Studying instructive intentional communities (Auroville, Mondragon), designing conflict resolution mechanisms before they're needed, clear bylaws and governance frameworks, trial periods for new members.</li>
    <li><strong>Legal risks</strong> — Engaging Bolivian legal counsel specializing in cooperative law, structuring ownership to comply with foreign ownership restrictions, maintaining relationships with local government, building community goodwill through local employment and shared infrastructure.</li>
  </ul>

